-- title:
--   The value functions $J_n(x,\rho)$ of the filtered terminal wealth model
-- statement:
--   Problem (6.2) is a stationary Partially Observable Markov Decision Model with $E_X=\operatorname{dom}U$, actions $a\in D(x)$, transition $T_X(x,a,z)=(1+i)(x+a\cdot z)$, $Q^{Z,Y}=Q^R\otimes Q^Y$, no running reward, terminal reward $U(x)$ and $\beta=1$. Its filtered Markov Decision Model has state space $E=E_X\times\mathbb P(E_Y)$; from state $(x,\rho)$ and action $a\in D(x)$ the relative risk $z$ is drawn from $\int Q^R(\cdot\mid y)\rho(dy)$ and the state moves to $\big((1+i)(x+a\cdot z),\Phi(\rho,z)\big)$; the terminal reward is $U(x)$.
--
--   The value functions of this model, with $n$ stages to go, are
--   $$
--   J_n(x,\rho)=\sup_{\pi}\ \mathbb E^\pi_{(x,\rho)}\,U(X_n),\qquad (x,\rho)\in E,
--   $$
--   the supremum over $n$-stage policies $\pi=(f_0,\dots,f_{n-1})$ of measurable decision rules $f_k:E\to\mathbb R^d$ with $f_k(x,\rho)\in D(x)$. In particular $J_0(x,\rho)=U(x)$. By Theorem 2.2.3 history-dependent policies of the filtered model would give the same value.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The expectation is taken under the law of the return path in the filtered model, along which the state is (wealth generated by the rules, filter). Values are extended reals. Decision rules are constrained only at wealth levels in $\operatorname{dom}U$.
-- source:
--   Bäuerle & Rieder (2011), §6.1, pp.176–177 (PDF 189–190); Definition 5.3.1, p.157 (PDF 170); value functions of Chapter 2, §2.2–2.3

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_PartialObs_Market_filteredLaw
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_PartialObs_markovWealth
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_PartialObs_Dset
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_RegimeSwitching_eint

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal Matrix

namespace BauerleRiederMDP.PartialObs

/-- **The value functions `J_n(x, ρ)` of the filtered terminal wealth model.**
Bäuerle & Rieder (2011), §6.1, pp.176–177 (PDF 189–190), and Definition 5.3.1, p.157 (PDF 170):
problem (6.2) is the stationary Partially Observable Markov Decision Model with `E_X = dom U`,
`A = ℝ^d`, `D(x)`, `T_X(x, a, z) = (1 + i)(x + a · z)`, `Q^{Z,Y} = Q^R ⊗ Q^Y`, `r ≡ 0`,
`g(x, y) = U(x)`, `β = 1`; its filtered model has state space `E = E_X × IP(E_Y)`, admissible
actions `D(x, ρ) = D(x)`, transition: `z ~ ∫ Q^R(·|y) ρ(dy)` and `(x, ρ) ↦ ((1 + i)(x + a · z),
Φ(ρ, z))`, no running reward and terminal reward `g'(x, ρ) = ∫ g(x, y) ρ(dy) = U(x)`.
The value functions of this model (as in Chapter 2, §2.2–2.3, with `n` stages to go) are

`J_n(x, ρ) = sup_π IE^π_{(x,ρ)} U(X_n)`,

the supremum over the `n`-stage policies `π = (f_0, …, f_{n−1})` of measurable decision rules
`f_k : E_X × IP(E_Y) → ℝ^d` with `f_k(x, ρ) ∈ D(x)` for all `(x, ρ) ∈ E` (Chapter 2: `F`, the set
of decision rules; by Theorem 2.2.3 history-dependent policies of the filtered model give the same
value). `J_0(x, ρ) = U(x)`.

**Formalization Note.** The expectation is taken under `M.filteredLaw ρ n`, the law of the
returns `(z_1, …, z_n)` in the filtered model, along which the state is
`(markovWealth M i x ρ f k, M.filter ρ k)`. Values are in `EReal` (`eint`). Decision rules are
functions on `ℝ × IP(E_Y)` and are only constrained at `x ∈ domU` (`E_X = dom U`). -/
noncomputable def J {EY : Type*} [MeasurableSpace EY] {d : ℕ} (M : Market EY d) (i : ℝ)
    (domU : Set ℝ) (U : ℝ → ℝ) (n : ℕ) (x : ℝ) (ρ : ProbabilityMeasure EY) : EReal :=
  ⨆ f ∈ {f : ℕ → ℝ × ProbabilityMeasure EY → Fin d → ℝ |
      ∀ k < n, Measurable (f k) ∧ ∀ x' ∈ domU, ∀ ρ' : ProbabilityMeasure EY,
        f k (x', ρ') ∈ Dset M i domU x'},
    BauerleRiederMDP.RegimeSwitching.eint (M.filteredLaw ρ n)
      (fun r => ((U (markovWealth M (fun _ => i) x ρ f n r) : ℝ) : EReal))

end BauerleRiederMDP.PartialObs


